1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,502
An EWMA chart has exactly one knob, and everything else follows from

2
00:00:05,602 --> 00:00:10,940
it. Each new subgroup is blended with everything that came before.

3
00:00:11,033 --> 00:00:15,621
Unroll the recursion and the weights fall away geometrically.

4
00:00:15,721 --> 00:00:18,850
At lambda one half the chart has almost no

5
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:22,693
memory: one subgroup back already counts for half.

6
00:00:22,783 --> 00:00:27,328
Turn it down and the tail grows: the chart starts remembering a

7
00:00:27,428 --> 00:00:31,899
dozen subgroups at a time, and reacts to none of them quickly.

8
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:35,933
Zero point two is the usual compromise: half

9
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:39,707
the weight is gone by 3.1 subgroups back.

10
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:45,352
That memory changes the limits themselves. A Shewhart chart

11
00:00:45,452 --> 00:00:49,056
holds the same three sigma either side, forever.

12
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:53,993
The EWMA limits start wide, because at subgroup one there is

13
00:00:54,093 --> 00:00:59,514
no accumulated evidence, then close as the weighted history builds.

14
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:03,206
They settle near one third of a sigma times three

15
00:01:03,306 --> 00:01:06,988
— a band 3.0 times narrower than the Shewhart one.

16
00:01:07,083 --> 00:01:11,354
The width comes from one expression, and at lambda zero

17
00:01:11,454 --> 00:01:14,135
point two it is a third of a sigma.

18
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:19,713
Tighter limits on smoothed data catch a small shift much earlier.

19
00:01:19,813 --> 00:01:25,293
Simulated at a matched false alarm rate: 44 subgroups against 10.

20
00:01:25,383 --> 00:01:29,039
4.4 times sooner, and lambda is the knob that

21
00:01:29,139 --> 00:01:32,795
decides how much of the past the chart keeps.

